## Planned regressions

Planned regression: output-only live seats retain the existing runner cwd but
receive byte-identical prompts that literally say OUTPUT-ONLY and forbid
checkout inspection; no GateAttempt grant is recorded.

Planned regression: opted-in live-run prompts literally authorize READ-ONLY
inspection of the recorded pin, a prompt-aware seat resolves the checkout
marker, and the resulting GateAttempt records read_checkout true.

Planned regression: opted-in supervised gate fails closed before any seat runs
with a clear live-run-only error; an output-only supervised gate still runs its
deterministic checks in the caller worktree.

Planned regression: before GH #14 integration, read_checkout true rejects a
pinned repository with an actual mode-160000 gitlink before any seat runs, with
a clear dependency error; a stray `.gitmodules` file without a gitlink does not
false-reject, and output-only gates remain unchanged.

Planned regression: every seat receives a byte-identical prompt containing the
authoritative-provenance, no-rederived-arithmetic, falsification-only, and
block-with-evidence instructions.

Planned regression: false/absent access prompts explicitly say output-only and
the gate record omits read_checkout.

The read-path test contains both policy branches: the same prompt-aware runner
does not inspect the marker under OUTPUT-ONLY and reads it under READ-ONLY,
proving the grant is load-bearing rather than merely checking prompt text.

Prompt assertions use independent literal strings written in the test; they do
not import a production rule constant or derive the oracle by calling the prompt
builder under test.

## Sequencing and disclosed limitation

KNOWN LIMITATION (disclosed, tracked): GitHub issue #14 is still open. Until it
lands, `worktree.Checkout` does not populate submodules or record their SHAs, so
this child supports only ordinary non-submodule repositories and rejects an
opted-in pinned tree containing a real gitlink before any seat runs. Output-only
gates remain unchanged. If #14 lands before final review, rebase, integrate its
manifest shape, replace the temporary rejection with the populated checkout,
and add the submodule regression. Otherwise the PR body must say submodule read
grants remain fail-closed pending #14. Reviewers should judge whether that is
acceptable scope for this PR-only deliverable or a blocker.

## L2 author declarations

TOPOLOGY: one gate orchestrator invokes seats sequentially today. The change
adds no concurrent writer or cwd. Seats keep the existing checkout cwd; the
shared prompt authorizes either output-only review or read-only inspection.

DURABILITY CLASS: no irreversible operation. Gate attempts retain the existing
at-most-once CAS append per round; the new boolean is part of that same manifest
write. No temporary checkout or cwd is added.

COMPLETE SET / all-sites enumeration: every seat-execution site is the single
`runGateSeats` helper; its two callers are `GateStage` and `runGate`. Every gate
record construction site is those same two callers. Every seat-prompt path is
enumerated above and must receive the rule. No other model-seat invocation is in
the liverun package.

TRUST BOUNDARY: workflow YAML and captured run manifests are untrusted until
their existing strict decoders validate them. The resolved boolean, captured
stage `git_sha`, and checkout returned by `worktree.Checkout` are trusted facts
rooted in those validators plus git object resolution. Stage output and model
seat conclusions are untrusted. Configured external seat harnesses are the
trusted enforcement boundary for registry modes and the prompt's access policy;
cwd is explicitly not an OS capability sandbox. Arbitrary same-process code or
a deliberately policy-violating configured command is outside scope.

IRREVERSIBLE SEQUENCES: none

